{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2196 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "fc58e607764f42665e08e4ed217d2b57115ea6fa461f5a345da8dfcc94929eaf" :edb/db-before-t 2195 :edb/db-after-t 2196 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535508 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:30:22.869Z" 13194139535508 true] [17592186175634 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the cone $z=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ that is subject to the constraints  $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 4,\\, x\\geq 0$ and $y\\geq 0.$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186175634 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the cone $z=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ that is subject to the constraints $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 4,\\, x\\geq 0$ and $y\\geq 0$." 13194139535508 false] [17592186175634 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: x^2+y^2\\leq 4, \\, x\\geq 0, \\, y\\geq 0 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3602/Source/Images/7230d57545aadd33786457bbde003627.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}, \\\\\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}) = \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}})^{2} + (\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{x^{2} + y^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region $D$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\sqrt{2}dA &= ∬_(Δ)\\sqrt{2}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}∫_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{2}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π/2}dθ∫_{0}^{2}rdr \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot [θ]_{0}^{π/2} \\cdot [\\frac{r^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{π}{2} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= π\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 false] [17592186175634 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x^2+y^2\\leq 4, \\, x\\geq 0, \\, y\\geq 0 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/be8246dc3bfe1bf16af5d9913fc4e6aef58ddad282646a00d3e31120f6bf31e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186175751 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $x =y+\\sqrt 2 z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by the constraints $1 \\leq y\\leq 2$ and $1 \\leq z \\leq 3$." 13194139535508 false] [17592186175751 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $x =y+\\sqrt 2 z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by the constraints $1 \\leq y\\leq 2$ and $1 \\leq z \\leq 3.$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186175751 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial y}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx = y+\\sqrt 2 z, \\qquad (y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 1 \\leq z \\leq 3\\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3602/Source/Images/573dc3290eb86abf9cb97e3dcfc181a6.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂x}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y + \\sqrt{2}z) = 1, \\\\\n\\frac{∂x}{∂z} &= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y + \\sqrt{2}z) = \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂x}{∂y})^{2} + (\\frac{∂x}{∂z})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (1)^{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{4}dA \\\\\n&= 2∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot Area(D) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (2 - 1) \\cdot (3 - 1) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 false] [17592186175751 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial y}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx = y+\\sqrt 2 z, \\qquad (y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 1 \\leq z \\leq 3\\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/240ac546b0dd725e392dd0bd475b2ef00eebce2594be5921285e227137538c46.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+\\sqrt{2}z)=1, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y+\\sqrt{2}z)=\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(1)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186175752 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z = y - 4x$ that is bounded by the constraints $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$." 13194139535508 false] [17592186175752 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z = y - 4x$ that is bounded by the constraints $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186175752 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z = f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = y - 4x, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq 1\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y - 4x) =-4, \\\\\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y - 4x) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (-4)^{2} + (1)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + 16 + 1}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{18}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)3\\sqrt{2}dA \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2}∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2} \\cdot Area(D) \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2} \\cdot (2 - 0) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 6\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 false] [17592186175752 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z = f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = y - 4x, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4x)=-4, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4x)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-4)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+16+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{18}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186175770 1100 "Find the surface area of the portion of the plane $z = 2y+2x$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\cos{x}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." 13194139535508 false] [17592186175770 1100 "Find the surface area of the portion of the plane $z = 2y+2x$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\cos{x}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186175770 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = 2y+2x, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\cos{x} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3602/Source/Images/8a755284c1de4b4761f2aaeff802f7d4.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y + 2x) = 2, \\\\\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y + 2x) = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (2)^{2} + (2)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)3dA \\\\\n&= 3∫_{0}^{π/2}∫_{0}^{\\cos x}dydx \\\\\n&= 3∫_{0}^{π/2}y\\begin{vmatrix}_{0}^{\\cos x}dx \\\\ = 3∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos xdx \\\\ = 3\\sin x\\end{vmatrix}_{0}^{π/2} \\\\\n&= 3(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 false] [17592186175770 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = 2y+2x, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\cos{x} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/e74d609a1288446f0f3adc0de65b814f2a6daaf2d398478e7834882f3be68c8c.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+2x)=2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+2x)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3(1-0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186175904 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352777 13194139535508 true] [17592186175904 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352784 13194139535508 true] [17592186175904 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352791 13194139535508 true] [17592186175904 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352798 13194139535508 true] [17592186175905 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352693 13194139535508 true] [17592186175905 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352700 13194139535508 true] [17592186175905 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352728 13194139535508 true] [17592186175905 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352735 13194139535508 true] [17592186175921 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352707 13194139535508 true] [17592186175921 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352714 13194139535508 true] [17592186175921 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352721 13194139535508 true] [17592186175921 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352770 13194139535508 true] [17592186175991 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352742 13194139535508 true] [17592186175991 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352749 13194139535508 true] [17592186175991 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352756 13194139535508 true] [17592186175991 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352763 13194139535508 true] [17592186176136 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535508 true] [17592186176136 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y = \\sin x + z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z = \\sin (2x)$ and the $x$-axis for $0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2}$." 13194139535508 false] [17592186176136 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186176136 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/be5dc3bf3ed4b6b7bee06d583b69e40a3922edede1e61a6f0b68d2ce0767f92a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{3}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186176164 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535508 true] [17592186176164 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z = 2x + 3y$ that is bounded by the constraints $0 \\le x \\le 1$ and $1 \\le y \\le 3$." 13194139535508 false] [17592186176164 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2x+3y$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186176164 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2x+3y,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)=2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+3y)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4+9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186176165 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535508 true] [17592186176165 1100 "Consider the portion of the paraboloid $z = x^{2} + y^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y = x$, $x = 1$, and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." 13194139535508 false] [17592186176165 1100 "Consider the portion of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y=x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." 13194139535508 true] [17592186176165 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/0b6de7829e702377d1b20bb1b297d3d28e6fb8bae9672b0137d92b4d53a96c83.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186176317 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535508 true] [17592186176317 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the paraboloid $x = 5 + y^{2} + z^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint $y^{2} + z^{2} \\le 3$." 13194139535508 false] [17592186176317 1100 "Find the surface area of the part of the paraboloid $x=5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 3.$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186176317 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx=5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2},\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 3},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/66b05919a7644818eea7265ff3ae8fc3ced4ac7d1d6d30352f612649393c876a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{13}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{13}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(13)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (13\\sqrt{13}-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352693 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352694 13194139535508 true] [17592186352693 1089 "q-161126" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352693 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535508 true] [17592186352693 1100 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352693 1113 #uuid "352914bd-a34c-5653-8a01-902adc8d34d8" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352693 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(4y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352695 13194139535508 true] [17592186352694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352696 13194139535508 true] [17592186352694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352697 13194139535508 true] [17592186352694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352698 13194139535508 true] [17592186352694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352699 13194139535508 true] [17592186352694 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535508 true] [17592186352694 1041 "selection" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352694 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352699 13194139535508 true] [17592186352694 1110 #uuid "a8d487cd-4f73-5fbf-8719-feeada2b20ce" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352695 1034 "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}}" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352695 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535508 true] [17592186352695 1111 #uuid "70adfa2d-4b67-584c-9484-97c32800a4ae" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352696 1034 "1+2{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352696 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535508 true] [17592186352696 1111 #uuid "11248fc3-e0c6-5a5b-a72c-5dd596495f80" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352697 1034 "\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352697 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535508 true] [17592186352697 1111 #uuid "7d3cbae4-2acc-5da0-b0ea-95b6888b7c37" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352698 1034 "1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352698 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535508 true] [17592186352698 1111 #uuid "509f76ce-102a-5af2-95df-16f2d47e8cee" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352699 1034 "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352699 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535508 true] [17592186352699 1111 #uuid "d8092ac4-a60f-5cde-937e-06dc44208de3" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352700 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352701 13194139535508 true] [17592186352700 1089 "q-161131" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352700 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535508 true] [17592186352700 1100 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352700 1113 #uuid "52890d5c-d18d-50d0-a821-47f1d481ca67" 13194139535508 true] [17592186352700 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={y}^{2}-{x}^{3},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2})}^{2}+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139535508 true] [175921863